<!--
    kuna strict fix (P6, ActionMarkImplied load-crosses-store test; no option).

    A load that reads bytes a later store overwrites must keep its own
    statement ahead of the store.  Whether a load may print after a store is
    decided by comparing the two pointers, and one base plus two different
    constants used to count as two objects whatever the access widths (upstream
    Ghidra's isPossibleAlias has the same hole).  So `inside` printed

        *(char *)(a0 + 8) = a1;
        return *(unsigned int *)(a0 + 7);

    which returns the new byte.  The test now asks whether the byte ranges
    [o1,o1+w1) and [o2,o2+w2) intersect.

    Source (gcc -O2 and clang -O2 emit the same pairs; endbr64 dropped):

      inside:  mov eax,[rdi+7]      / mov [rdi+8],sil        byte into the read
      below:   mov eax,[rdi+7]      / mov [rdi+5],esi        4 bytes below it
      indexed: mov rax,[rdi+rsi*4]  / mov [rdi+rsi*4+4],edx  element into an 8-byte read
      after:   mov eax,[rdi+7]      / mov [rdi+0xb],sil      adjacent byte after
      before:  mov eax,[rdi+7]      / mov [rdi+6],sil        adjacent byte before
      next:    mov eax,[rdi+rsi*4]  / mov [rdi+rsi*4+4],edx  adjacent element

    One pass, defaults only.  #1 and #2: inside, below and indexed read into a
    local ahead of the store (before the fix all three printed the read after
    it, and #1, #2 and #3 fail).  #3 and #4: after, before and next touch no
    read byte and keep the read folded into the return, as before.
-->
